From 8386a7b78a65293929489cb12375d3bef4ecc7dc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nimit <nimit@local>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 23:54:05 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] Let post_tool rewrite tool output, opt-in and fail-open

`post_tool` is an observer and its stdout is discarded, so nothing outside
jcode can reduce what a tool result contributes to the context window.
`pre_tool` can gate a call but only by blocking it. That leaves no way to
compress a noisy build log or redact a secret a command printed.

`hooks.post_tool_filter = true` makes post_tool synchronous: it receives the
tool output on stdin and its stdout replaces that output. Exit 0 with empty
stdout means "no opinion" and is the common case for a filter that only
handles some tools. A non-zero exit, a timeout, invalid UTF-8, or a missing
binary all keep the original, matching the reasoning already written down for
pre_tool: a broken policy script should degrade to "no policy" rather than
brick a session. Here it costs a compression, never a tool result.

Off by default. An observer can never slow a turn, and that property is worth
keeping unless the user asks otherwise.

Runs before guard_context_overflow, so a hook that shrinks a large payload can
keep it under the limit instead of being truncated first. The filtered form is
what enters the transcript, so it is also what gets resent every later turn,
which is where the saving actually accrues in an agent loop.

Output may grow, bounded. A strict "must not grow" rule rejected redaction,
which is one of the two obvious uses: "[redacted]" is longer than the secret
it replaces. The ceiling is a slack multiple instead, which still stops a
runaway hook from inflating context without limit.

Verified end to end against a forked binary: a 400-line build log emitted by
the real bash tool reached the hook, came back as 8 lines with an explicit
elision marker, and jcode used the filtered form. 15 hooks tests pass.
